<div class="section-header" id="salsa">

    <span class="section-kicker">
        SALSA
    </span>

    <h2>
        Why the World Calls Cali the Salsa Capital
    </h2>

</div>

<p>
    Contrary to what many visitors believe, salsa was not born in Cali. By the time the genre reached Colombia, it had already emerged from a mix of Caribbean musical traditions, particularly Cuban forms, and was shaped by Puerto Rican and other Caribbean communities in New York during the 20th century.
</p>

<p>
    What happened in Cali was something entirely different. The city didn't invent salsa: it made it its own. More importantly, it developed a distinctive way of dancing it, known today as <strong>salsa caleña</strong>.
</p>

<p>
    Since salsa first arrived here, generations of caleños have continuously reinvented the dance, creating increasingly fast, creative and footwork-focused styles that you simply won't find anywhere else in the world. Watching a couple dance in Cali is very different from watching salsa in Cuba, Puerto Rico or New York.
</p>

<p>
    We  believe salsa found fertile ground in Cali during a period of profound social change. As thousands of people migrated to the city (especially Afro-Colombian communities from Colombia's Pacific region) the city's cultural identity evolved rapidly. Combined with Buenaventura, Colombia's main Pacific port and the gateway through which countless vinyl records entered the country, Cali developed an extraordinary connection with Afro-Caribbean music.
</p>

<div class="editorial-quote">

<p>
    Cali didn't become the world's salsa capital because it produced the most famous salsa bands.
</p>

<h3>
    It became the world's salsa capital because it reinvented the way the world dances salsa.
</h3>

<p class="editorial-quote__footer">
    Generations of extraordinary dancers, passionate record collectors and music lovers transformed salsa into something that became part of everyday life.
</p>

</div>


<p> Today, salsa is far more than something you'll see on stage. It's woven into everyday life. You'll hear it in taxis, neighborhood stores, family gatherings, cafés and parks. And if you happen to visit during late December, you'll experience the <strong>Feria de Cali</strong>, the city's biggest celebration and one of Colombia's most important festivals. For 5 days, Cali becomes a giant dance floor where locals and visitors celebrate the music that has shaped the city's identity for generations. </p>

<p> Beyond the dancers, Cali is also home to a remarkable and passionate community of <strong>melómanos</strong>: music lovers and vinyl collectors who have spent decades preserving, studying and sharing salsa recordings from across Latin America and the Caribbean. Their passion has helped keep the genre alive, influencing generations of dancers and making recorded music an essential part of Cali's salsa culture. </p>

<p> Whether you already dance or have never taken a single lesson, experiencing salsa in Cali is one of the best ways to understand the city itself. It isn't simply entertainment: it is one of the languages through which Cali expresses its history, diversity and personality. </p>

<div class="section-header" id="nightlife">

    <span class="section-kicker">
        NIGHTLIFE
    </span>

    <h2>
        Nightlife in Cali
    </h2>

</div>

<p>
    Cali may not have Colombia's most expensive nightlife scene, but it has something many visitors find more valuable: nightlife that is deeply connected to local culture.
</p>

<p>
    Much of this culture comes from salsa. For generations, music and dancing have been part of everyday life, making nightlife feel less like an activity for visitors and more like a natural extension of the city's personality. Another reason is the region itself. For dozens of kilometers surrounding Cali, the landscape is dominated by sugar cane plantations, making Valle del Cauca one of Colombia's traditional producers of sugar cane spirits, especially <strong>aguardiente</strong>. Sharing a bottle with friends remains a common part of a night out.
</p>

<p>
    Visitors are often surprised by how approachable locals can be. Caleños are generally warm, curious and genuinely interested in meeting people from other countries. Don't be surprised if someone asks where you're from or invites you to dance salsa. More often than not, they're simply being friendly.
</p>

<div class="editorial-quote">

    <p>
        Cali doesn't party for tourists.
    </p>

    <h3>
        Tourists are simply invited to join the party.
    </h3>

</div>

<p>
    Like any large city, common-sense precautions still apply. Keep an eye on your belongings, avoid accepting drinks from strangers, and use trusted transportation late at night. Overall, Cali has a relaxed nightlife atmosphere.
</p>

<p>
    Broadly speaking, you'll find two different nightlife experiences. One is spontaneous and very local: open-air venues, lively streets, salsa bars and neighborhoods where people naturally move from one place to another. The other is a more upscale scene, with elegant clubs, rooftop bars and stylish venues where people dress up and enjoy a more sophisticated atmosphere.
</p>

<p>
    Some areas are naturally more popular with international visitors, while others remain almost entirely local. Both offer fantastic experiences for different reasons, and choosing where to go depends on the kind of night you're looking for.
</p>

<div class="section-header" id="nature">

    <span class="section-kicker">
        NATURE
    </span>

    <h2>
        Nature around Cali
    </h2>

</div>

<p>
    One of the biggest surprises for first-time visitors is how naturally Cali blends into its surroundings. While many cities treat nature as something you have to drive hours to reach, in Cali it simply becomes part of everyday life. Cali sits in the Cauca River valley on the eastern slopes of Colombia's Western Andes, with the Pacific lowlands and rainforest of Valle del Cauca not far to the west, one of the most biodiverse places on Earth, making wildlife and green landscapes an ordinary part of life for locals.
</p>

<p>
    Every afternoon, usually past 4 p.m. and before dark, flocks of parrots and macaws fly across the city, filling the air with their unmistakable calls. Wherever there are tall palm trees, chances are you'll hear them. Colombia is home to more species of palm trees than any other country in the world, and Cali benefits from that extraordinary natural richness.
</p>

<div class="editorial-quote">

    <p>
        In Cali, nature isn't somewhere you go.
    </p>

    <h3>
        It's simply part of everyday life.
    </h3>

</div>

<p>
    The Cali River flows directly through the city and it is even home to a small population of wild river otters, something few visitors expect to find in the middle of a city of more than two million people.
</p>

<p>
    Birdwatchers from around the world travel to Colombia, and Cali has quietly become one of the country's best gateways into that world. Within an hour from central Cali, the cloud forests of the Farallones de Cali National Park are home to extraordinary species, including the Multicolored Tanager (Chlorochrysa nitidissima), an endemic bird of Colombia. The region is also famous for its hummingbirds. Within a short distance from the city you can see birds you may have never seen before.
</p>

<p>
    For locals, these mountains are more than a birdwatching destination. They're where people escape the tropical heat. After just a short drive up the mountains, temperatures drop dramatically, the air becomes cooler, and traditional countryside lodges and glampings offer one of the most peaceful experiences near the city.
</p>

<p>
    Back in the city, the Cali Zoo sits in a leafy area and is home to a wide variety of Colombian wildlife.
</p>

<p>
    No guide to nature around Cali would be complete without mentioning Pance. Every weekend, locals head to its crystal clear mountain river to cool off, relax with family and friends, or, as many jokingly admit, recover from a Saturday night filled with salsa and aguardiente. The water is refreshing, coming directly from the streams flowing down from the mountains.
</p>

<p>
    And if you have an extra day, San Cipriano is one of the most unforgettable destinations in the region. Surrounded by dense tropical rainforest, this small village showcases why Colombia is considered one of the world's biodiversity hotspots. Birds such as the spectacular oropendola are commonly seen around the village, while rivers, jungle trails and the famous motorcycle-powered 'brujitas' rail carts create an experience unlike anywhere.
</p>

<p>
    Whether you're interested in birdwatching, waterfalls, hiking, cloud forests, mountain rivers or simply escaping the city for a few hours, Cali offers a side of Colombia that many travelers never expect to find.
</p>

<div class="section-header" id="food">

<span class="section-kicker">
    FOOD
</span>

<h2>
    Food in Cali, Colombia
</h2>

</div>

<p>
    If you're wondering what to eat in Cali, Colombia, the answer goes far beyond a list of traditional dishes. The food of Cali tells the story of the people who built the city: Indigenous communities, Spanish settlers, Afro-Colombian communities from the Pacific Coast, and generations of migrants who brought their own ingredients, techniques and traditions.
</p>

<p>
    Valle del Cauca is one of Colombia's great agricultural regions, and Cali sits at the center of this extraordinary culinary crossroads. The result is a cuisine that is difficult to define with a single style. Instead, <strong>traditional Cali food</strong> reflects the geography and cultural diversity of southwestern Colombia.
</p>

<div class="editorial-quote">

<p>
    Cali's cuisine isn't defined by a single tradition.
</p>

<h3>
    It's the result of centuries of cultures sharing the same table.
</h3>

</div>

<h3>
    Traditional Food from Cali and Valle del Cauca
</h3>

<p>
    One of the strongest influences on local cuisine comes from the sugar cane plantations of Valle del Cauca. The region's long history of sugar cane production helped shape some of Cali's most beloved sweets and desserts.
</p>

<p>
    <strong>Manjar blanco</strong>, a creamy milk-based sweet similar in some ways to arequipe (colombian dulce de leche), is one of the region's best-known traditional foods. Other local specialties include <strong>dulce cortado</strong> and <strong>desamargado</strong>, traditional sweets that are particularly associated with Christmas, family celebrations and the culinary traditions of Valle del Cauca.
</p>

<p>
    These aren't simply desserts for tourists to try. They are foods that many caleños grew up eating at home and that continue to appear on family tables and during important celebrations.
</p>

<h3>
    Pacific Colombian Cuisine in Cali
</h3>

<p>
    Another essential part of understanding food in Cali is the city's connection with Colombia's Pacific Coast. Cali has received generations of migration from Pacific communities, particularly Afro-Colombian communities, and their influence is deeply embedded in the city's food culture.
</p>

<p>
    This is especially noticeable in seafood. Pacific Colombian cuisine makes extensive use of fresh fish, shellfish, coconut, herbs and tropical ingredients, and dishes inspired by this tradition can be found throughout Cali.
</p>

<p>
    Traditional markets such as <strong>Galeria Alameda</strong> are particularly interesting places to experience this side of Cali's gastronomy. You'll find fresh ingredients, seafood, juices, prepared foods and restaurants serving dishes influenced by the Pacific region.
</p>

<p>
    This connection between Cali and the Pacific is one of the reasons the city's food scene feels so different from what many visitors experience in other parts of Colombia.
</p>

<h3>
    Tropical Fruits and Traditional Drinks
</h3>

<p>
    The biodiversity of southwestern Colombia also makes Cali an incredible place to discover tropical fruits that many international visitors have never tasted before.
</p>

<p>
    Fruits such as <strong>chirimoya, naidí, motilón</strong> and cacao are part of the wider regional food culture, appearing in juices, desserts and other preparations. Some are familiar throughout Colombia, while others are much more closely associated with specific regions and ecosystems.
</p>

<p>
    Two of the most iconic things to try in Cali are <strong>cholado</strong> and <strong>lulada</strong>.
</p>

<p>
    <strong>Cholado</strong> is a colorful dessert made with shaved ice, fresh fruit, syrups and condensed milk. It's particularly popular on hot afternoons and is as much a part of Cali's street-food culture as it is a dessert.
</p>

<p>
    <strong>Lulada</strong> is a refreshing drink made with fresh lulo, ice, sugar and condensed milk. The fruit is crushed rather than completely blended, giving the drink its characteristic texture. On a warm Cali afternoon, it is difficult to imagine a more local way to cool down.
</p>

<h3>
    Cali Street Food and Fritanga
</h3>

<p>
    If you want to understand everyday <strong>Cali Colombian food</strong>, don't limit yourself to restaurants. Some of the city's most beloved foods are inexpensive snacks that people eat throughout the day.
</p>

<p>
    Cali's <strong>fritanga</strong> culture includes foods such as <strong>empanadas, marranitas, papa rellena</strong> and <strong>aborrajados</strong>. You'll find them in bakeries, neighborhood shops, markets and street-food stalls, often for just a few thousand pesos.
</p>

<p>
    <strong>Marranitas</strong>, made with green (or ripe) plantain and pork, are a particularly distinctive specialty of Valle del Cauca. <strong>Aborrajados</strong> combine ripe plantain with cheese and a fried batter, creating the sweet-and-savory combination that characterizes so much of the region's comfort food.
</p>

<p>
    And then there is the <strong>chuleta valluna</strong>: a breaded and fried pork cutlet that has become one of the best-known dishes associated with Valle del Cauca. It is simple, filling and very much part of the region's everyday food culture.
</p>

<h3>
    Cali's Bakeries, Restaurants and Modern Food Scene
</h3>

<p>
    Traditional food is only part of the story. Cali also has a growing restaurant scene where local ingredients and Colombian traditions are being reinterpreted in more contemporary ways.
</p>

<p>
    You'll find everything from neighborhood bakeries and traditional lunch restaurants to specialty coffee shops, international cuisine, tasting menus and contemporary Colombian restaurants.
</p>

<p>
    Some of the city's most interesting food neighborhoods include <strong>San Antonio, El Peñón, Parque del Perro, Granada</strong> and <strong>Ciudad Jardín</strong>. Each offers a different atmosphere, from casual local restaurants to more sophisticated dining experiences.
</p>

<p>
    One of the great advantages of eating in Cali is value. A simple local meal can be remarkably affordable, while even higher-end restaurants are often considerably less expensive than comparable experiences in many North American or European cities.
</p>

<h3>
    Food as a Way to Understand Cali
</h3>

<p>
    Ultimately, the best food in Cali isn't necessarily found in the most famous restaurant or the most expensive neighborhood. Some of the most memorable things you'll eat may be a freshly fried empanada, a glass of lulada, a plate of chuleta valluna or a dessert bought from someone who has been making it the same way for decades.
</p>

<p>
    Food is one of the easiest ways to understand Cali's history. Sugar cane connects the city to the agricultural valley surrounding it. Pacific seafood reflects generations of migration and Afro-Colombian culture. Tropical fruits connect Cali to the extraordinary biodiversity of southwestern Colombia. And street food shows how these traditions became part of everyday life.
</p>

<p>
    So if you're asking <strong>what to eat in Cali, Colombia</strong>, don't think of the answer as a checklist. Come hungry, explore the markets and neighborhood restaurants, try things you don't recognize, and let the city's food tell you a little about where it came from.
</p>


<div class="section-header" id="afro-culture">

    <span class="section-kicker">
        AFRO CULTURE
    </span>

    <h2>
        Afro-Colombian Culture
    </h2>

</div>

<p>
    It is impossible to understand Cali without understanding its Afro-Colombian heritage. The city is home to one of the largest Afro-descendant urban population in Colombia (and in Latinamerica) by absolute number, making Black culture not a niche or minority expression, but one of the foundations of everyday life.
</p>

<p>
    For many visitors from the African diaspora, especially from North America and the Caribbean, this creates an immediate sense of familiarity. It is common to hear visitors say that Cali is one of the few places where they feel they can blend in rather than stand out.
</p>

<div class="editorial-quote">

    <p>
        To understand Cali...
    </p>

    <h3>
        ...you first have to understand its Afro-Colombian soul.
    </h3>

</div>

<p>
    Diversity is simply part of everyday life here. Music, language, dance, cuisine and even humor have all been profoundly shaped by Afro-Colombian communities, particularly those whose families migrated from Colombia's Pacific Coast throughout the twentieth century.
</p>

<p>
    Locals recognize a famous phrase from the classic salsa song <strong>"Sin negro no hay guaguancó"</strong>, recorded by the <strong>Lebrón Brothers</strong> on their album <em>Criollo</em>.
</p>

<div class="youtube-embed">

<iframe
    width="560"
    height="315"
    src="https://www.youtube.com/embed/-jJNyhjou_w?si=5va23WPL4WroW6zL"
