</div>

<p>
    Literally, the phrase means "Without Black people there is no party." But in Cali, the idea carries a much deeper cultural meaning: Afro-descendant communities have played a fundamental role in shaping the music, dance, food and social culture that make the city what it is today.
</p>

<p>
    Cali's Afro-descendant population traces its roots back centuries to the colonial period and the transatlantic slave trade. Much of their original African heritage was intentionally suppressed during colonization, yet countless traditions survived and evolved. They remain visible today in music, dance, food, language, spirituality and countless everyday customs.
</p>

<p>
    Their influence extends throughout the city, but becomes especially visible in eastern Cali, where many neighborhoods have predominantly Afro-Colombian populations and where cultural traditions continue to thrive through music, family life and community celebrations.
</p>

<p>
    Whether you're dancing salsa, listening to Pacific music, enjoying traditional seafood or simply walking through the city, you're experiencing a culture that has been profoundly shaped by generations of Afro-Colombians whose contributions continue to define Cali's identity.
</p>

</div>

<div class="section-header" id="where-to-stay">

    <span class="section-kicker">
        WHERE TO STAY
    </span>

    <h2>
        Where to Stay in Cali
    </h2>

</div>

<p>
    Choosing where to stay in Cali is probably one of the most important decisions you'll make before your trip. The city has more than two million inhabitants, and the overwhelming majority of its neighborhoods are residential, where everyday local life unfolds far away from the tourist areas.
</p>

<p>
    That creates two very different experiences. You can stay in Cali's traditional visitor neighborhoods, where restaurants, museums, nightlife and historic landmarks are often within walking distance. Or you can choose a more residential area, offering a much more authentic local experience, usually at a lower price, but requiring Uber rides to reach most attractions.
</p>

<div class="editorial-quote">

    <p>
        The best neighborhood doesn't exist.
    </p>

    <h3>
        The best neighborhood depends entirely on the kind of trip you want to have.
    </h3>

</div>

<h3>Historic Cali</h3>

<p>
    For most first-time visitors, this is where we'd recommend staying. San Antonio, El Peñón and Granada form the traditional tourist heart of the city, combining history, restaurants, nightlife, boutique hotels and easy access to many of Cali's main attractions.
</p>

<p>
    <strong>San Antonio</strong> is perhaps Cali's most iconic neighborhood. Colonial houses, quiet streets, cafés and panoramic viewpoints create one of the city's most charming atmospheres. A large concentration of backpacker hostels are located here, alongside beautiful Airbnbs and boutique hotels.
</p>

<p>
    <strong>El Peñón</strong>, immediately next to San Antonio, feels slightly more modern. Here you'll find international restaurants, cocktail bars, upscale accommodation and some of Cali's best hotels and Airbnbs.
</p>

<p>
    <strong>Granada</strong> is another favorite. Walkable and filled with restaurants, cafés, boutique hotels and nightlife, it also hosts international hotel chains together with excellent independent hotels.
</p>

<p><strong>Best for:</strong> First-time visitors • Walkability • Restaurants • Salsa • Nightlife</p>

<hr>

<h3>Medical & Luxury Cali</h3>

<p>
    Ciudad Jardín & Pance are particularly popular with visitors looking for upscale accommodation and travelling to Cali for dental treatments or medical tourism. It is home to numerous private clinics, luxury restaurants, shopping malls and elegant residential developments.
</p>

<p>
    Unlike Historic Cali, however, Ciudad Jardín & Pance are built around the car. Distances are much greater, making Uber almost essential for sightseeing. On the other hand, it offers quick access to Pance river, waterfalls, the mountains of the Farallones de Cali National Natural Park, prestigious universities and event venues.
</p>

<p>
    The area is also known for its large mansions, some of which have been transformed into luxury hotels, recovery houses and wellness facilities.
</p>

<p><strong>Best for:</strong> Medical tourism • Luxury • Families • Longer stays</p>

<hr>

<h3>Local Favorites</h3>

<p>
    If you've already visited Cali once (or simply want to experience the city like a local) neighborhoods such as San Fernando, Santa Teresita, area around Parque del Perro, Versalles and Centenario offer an excellent balance between local life and proximity to the city's attractions.
</p>

<p>
    These neighborhoods feel less touristy while remaining conveniently located. You'll find charming boutique hotels, excellent restaurants, parks and cafés without the crowds of San Antonio or Granada.
</p>

<p>
    Versalles and Centenario offer excellent options both from international to local hotels. Nearby Chipichape Shopping Mall also hosts two of Cali's largest hotels.
</p>

<p>
    Tequendama deserves a special mention thanks to its proximity to the Coliseo del Pueblo, the Petronio Álvarez Festival, the World Salsa Festival, Cali Fair and Mall Plaza.
</p>

<p><strong>Best for:</strong> Returning visitors • Digital nomads • Local atmosphere</p>

<hr>

<h3>Residential Cali</h3>

<p>
    Many neighborhoods across northern, central and southern Cali offer a wonderfully authentic experience for visitors staying several weeks or even months. Areas such as La Flora, Prados del Norte, Alameda, Colseguros, El Ingenio, El Refugio and Cuarto de Legua provide excellent value and allow you to experience daily life far from the traditional tourist circuit.
</p>

<p>
    Accommodation is often more affordable, although you'll likely spend more on Uber rides as most attractions are not within walking distance.
</p>

<p><strong>Best for:</strong> Long stays • Living like a local • Budget-conscious travelers</p>

<hr>

<h3>Areas We Don't Recommend for First-Time Visitors</h3>

<p>
    Like any large city, some neighborhoods are simply better suited for visitors than others. For first-time visitors, we generally wouldn't choose accommodation in Downtown, Siloé, Meléndez or much of eastern Cali unless you have a specific reason to stay there or are familiar with the area. These areas can certainly be visited, but they are less convenient bases for most first-time travelers.
</p>

<div class="section-header" id="transportation">

    <span class="section-kicker">
        TRANSPORTATION
    </span>

    <h2>
        How to Get Around Cali, Colombia
    </h2>

</div>

<p>
    Getting around Cali, Colombia is easier than many first-time visitors expect. The city has no metro or passenger train, but you can combine walking, taxis, ride-hailing apps and the MIO public transportation system depending on where you're staying and how much time you have.
</p>

<p>
    For most travelers, the simplest combination is <strong>walking in the traditional tourist neighborhoods</strong> and using <strong>Uber or another ride-hailing app</strong> for longer journeys between neighborhoods.
</p>

<h3>
    Is Uber Available in Cali, Colombia?
</h3>

<p>
    Yes. <strong>Uber is widely used in Cali</strong>, and ride-hailing is one of the most convenient ways for international visitors to get around the city.
</p>

<p>
    Uber operates in a complicated legal environment in Colombia. The issue is primarily the legal status of the vehicle providing the ride: Colombian regulations distinguish between private and public transportation, while ride-hailing platforms commonly connect passengers with privately registered vehicles.
</p>

<p>
    In everyday life, however, ride-hailing apps are extremely common. You may also see drivers using <strong>DiDi</strong> or <strong>inDrive</strong>. No single app is always the cheapest or fastest, so comparing prices and availability can be worthwhile.
</p>

<p>
    One local custom may surprise you. Your driver may ask you to sit in the front passenger seat. This is related to the complicated regulatory environment surrounding app-based transportation and is intended to reduce the driver's risk during traffic checks.
</p>

<p>
    Don't take it personally if you're asked to move forward. It's a fairly common practice.
</p>

<p>
    One practical warning: <strong>don't request your ride at the last possible minute</strong>. Finding a driver can take as long as 15 minutes, even when it's not rush hour. If you have a tour, restaurant reservation, appointment or flight connection, give yourself some extra time.
</p>

<div class="editorial-quote">

    <p>
        <strong>My local rule after years of guiding:</strong>
    </p>

    <h3>
        If you have somewhere important to be, budget time to find the car, not just time for the drive.
    </h3>

</div>

<h3>
    Taxis in Cali
</h3>

<p>
    <strong>Taxis are everywhere in Cali</strong> and remain a normal and legitimate way to get around. Locals use them every day, and they can be particularly useful when you need a car immediately.
</p>

<p>
    Taxis use a taximeter, although legally established surcharges can affect the final fare. For visitors, the main advantage of using an app instead is price and trip transparency: you can normally see the route, fare information and payment method before or during the journey.
</p>

<p>
    You can hail a taxi on the street, but international visitors may find ride-hailing apps easier to navigate, particularly if they don't speak much Spanish.
</p>

<h3>
    Walking Around Cali
</h3>

<p>
    <strong>Cali is very walkable in the right neighborhoods.</strong> If you're staying in San Antonio, El Peñón or Granada, many restaurants, cafés, bars, parks and attractions are close enough to reach on foot.
</p>

<p>
    This is one of the biggest advantages of staying in the traditional visitor areas. You can spend part of the day exploring on foot and then use a ride-hailing app when you need to cross the city.
</p>

<p>
    Farther south, particularly around Ciudad Jardín and Pance, the experience is different. Distances are greater and the neighborhoods are much more car-oriented.
</p>

<h3>
    How Does the MIO Work?
</h3>

<p>
    The <strong>MIO</strong> is Cali's integrated public transportation system and the city's main public bus network. It includes dedicated bus corridors and stations connecting large parts of Cali.
</p>

<p>
    Visitors can use a traditional MIO card, or, where supported, pay by tapping a contactless Visa or Mastercard on the validator.
</p>

<p>
    You don't need to understand the entire network before using it. Google Maps can be particularly useful for finding MIO routes, stations and transfers: enter your destination, choose public transportation and follow the suggested route.
</p>

<p>
    The main disadvantage is travel time. A journey that takes relatively little time by car can take considerably longer on the MIO when waiting and transfers are included.
</p>

<p>
    If you're staying in Cali for several days and want to experience the city more like a local, using the MIO can be an interesting part of the experience. For a short trip, however, many visitors will prefer the convenience of ride-hailing.
</p>

<h3>
    Is Public Transportation Safe in Cali?
</h3>

<p>
    The MIO is part of everyday life for millions of people in Cali, but visitors should use the same precautions they would use on public transportation in any large Latin American city.
</p>

<p>
    Keep your phone and valuables secure, particularly in crowded stations and buses, avoid displaying expensive belongings and pay attention to your surroundings. At night, many visitors will find a ride-hailing app more convenient than waiting for public transportation or walking long distances.
</p>

<p>
    Safety also depends heavily on where you are going and when. There isn't a single rule that applies to every neighborhood or every time of day.
</p>

<div class="editorial-quote">

    <p>
        <strong>A useful local principle:</strong>
    </p>

    <h3>
        Choose your transportation based on the neighborhood, the time of day and how familiar you are with the area.
    </h3>

</div>

<h3>
    How to Get from Cali Airport to the City
</h3>

<p>
    <strong>Alfonso Bonilla Aragón International Airport (CLO)</strong>, Cali's main international airport, is actually located outside the city near Palmira.
</p>

<p>
    There is no airport metro or passenger train connecting the airport directly with Cali. Public and shared transportation options are available, but routes and fares can change, so travelers carrying luggage will generally find a taxi or ride-hailing service more convenient.
</p>

<p>
    Official airport taxis are readily available. Uber pickups are also possible, although the exact pickup procedure can depend on the airport's current arrangements and your driver.
</p>

<p>
    Don't be surprised if transportation to and from the airport costs noticeably more than a typical ride within Cali.
</p>

<h3>
    Getting Around Cali from Ciudad Jardín and Pance
</h3>

<p>
    If you're staying in <strong>Ciudad Jardín or Pance</strong>, transportation deserves some extra planning.
</p>

<p>
    These southern areas are popular with visitors staying longer, people coming for medical or dental treatment, families and travelers looking for a quieter environment close to nature. They are also considerably farther from the traditional tourist areas than San Antonio, El Peñón and Granada.
</p>

<p>
    Cali may not look enormous on a map, but traffic can make journeys much longer than the distance suggests. An activity that looks "close" geographically may still require a substantial drive.
</p>

<p>
    If you're staying in Ciudad Jardín or Pance, check the actual travel time to your tours and activities before booking accommodation. This is particularly important if you have early-morning departures or several activities planned across different parts of the city.
</p>

<h3>
    Should You Rent a Car in Cali?
</h3>

<p>
    <strong>Yes, you can rent a car in Cali but most short-term visitors don't need one.</strong>
</p>

<p>
    International rental companies operate in the city, and having your own vehicle can be useful for longer stays or trips into the surrounding countryside.
</p>

<p>
    Within Cali itself, however, traffic can be challenging. Motorcycles are common, lane discipline may feel different from what you're accustomed to, and parking can become an additional headache.
</p>

<p>
    For travelers who have never driven in Colombia, the convenience of having a rental car may not outweigh the stress of navigating an unfamiliar driving environment.
</p>

<p>
    For most visitors staying only a few days, walking and ride-hailing apps will usually be simpler and more convenient.
</p>

<h3>
    So, What's the Best Way to Get Around Cali?
</h3>

<p>
    There isn't one transportation option that works for every traveler. Your best choice depends mainly on where you're staying and how much time you have.
</p>

<p>
    <strong>Walking</strong> is ideal in San Antonio, El Peñón and Granada.
</p>

<p>
    <strong>Uber and other ride-hailing apps</strong> are generally the easiest way to move between neighborhoods.
</p>

<p>
    <strong>Taxis</strong> are widely available and useful when you need immediate transportation.
</p>

<p>
    <strong>The MIO</strong> is affordable and extensive, but usually slower.
</p>

<p>
    <strong>Renting a car</strong> makes more sense for longer stays and trips outside the city than for a short urban visit.
</p>

<p>
    For most first-time visitors to Cali, we'd recommend keeping it simple: <strong>walk when you're in a good neighborhood for walking and use ride-hailing when you're not.</strong>
</p>

<section id="how-many-days">
<div class="section-header">
<span class="section-kicker">
    HOW MANY DAYS?
</span>
<h2>
    How Many Days Should You Spend in Cali, Colombia?
</h2>
</div>
<p>
    You can get a taste of Cali in one day, and many travelers actually do. After years of working in tourism, I've learned that spending only one or two days in Cali is surprisingly common, especially for visitors following a larger Colombia itinerary.
</p>

<p>
    Personally, I don't think one day is enough to really experience the city. Even two days can feel short. But I also don't want to pretend that every traveler needs five days in Cali. <strong>For a first visit, three days is the sweet spot I'd recommend.</strong>
</p>

<p>
    Three days gives you enough time to experience Cali's historic neighborhoods, food, salsa and nightlife while still leaving room for the mountains, rivers or one of the natural experiences that make the city so different from other destinations in Colombia.
</p>

<p>
    Here's how we'd actually use your time depending on how many days you have.
</p>

<h3>
    One Day in Cali
</h3>

<p>
    With only one day, you'll need to be selective. Fortunately, Cali's combination of city and nature makes it possible to experience a surprising amount in a short time.
</p>

<p>
    Start around 8 a.m. at <strong>Galería Alameda</strong>, one of the city's most interesting traditional markets. It's a great introduction to Colombian fruits, local ingredients and the everyday food culture of Cali.
</p>

<p>
    From there, head toward Pance and spend the late morning visiting the <strong>Indio Waterfall</strong>. Cool off in the mountain river before having a traditional <strong>sancocho de gallina</strong> for lunch at <strong>El Portal de Pance</strong>.
</p>

<p>
    After lunch, return toward the city for an afternoon of culture. Depending on your interests, you could visit the <strong>Jairo Varela Museum</strong> or the <strong>Museo de la Salsa</strong>, before heading up to <strong>Cristo Rey</strong> in the late afternoon for sunset and one of the best views over Cali.
</p>

<p>
    Finish the day with dinner in San Antonio. If you're visiting on a weekend, continue toward the Bulevar del Río and the <strong>Calle de la Salsa</strong>, or experience one of Cali's many salsa clubs and nightlife venues.
</p>

<p>
    One day can work. It just won't give you much room to slow down.
</p>

<p>
    If you'd rather have someone organize the logistics, our <a href="/tours/galeria-alameda">Galería Alameda Tour</a>, <a href="/tours/pance-waterfalls">Pance Waterfalls Tour</a>, <a href="/tours/cristo-rey-city-tour">Cristo Rey City Tour</a> and <a href="/tours/salsa-tour">Salsa Tour</a> can be combined to create a very full first day.
</p>

<h3>
    Two Days in Cali
</h3>

<p>
    Two days gives you considerably more breathing room and is probably the most common length of stay we see among travelers passing through Cali.
</p>

<p>
    Spend your first day exploring the city itself: its historic neighborhoods, local food, viewpoints, museums and salsa culture. Save the evening for dinner and nightlife, particularly if you're visiting on a Friday or Saturday.
</p>

<p>
    On your second day, add one of the experiences that best demonstrates just how close nature is to Cali. You could visit the <strong>cloud forest of the Farallones de Cali National Natural Park</strong>, visit a hummingbird sanctuary, or explore the mountains and rivers around Pance.
</p>

<p>
    Two days can give you a good introduction to Cali. You'll still have to leave some things for another trip.
</p>

<h3>
    Three Days in Cali: The Sweet Spot
</h3>

<p>
    <strong>Three days is what we'd recommend for most first-time visitors.</strong>
</p>

<p>
    It gives you enough time to experience the city's main cultural highlights without turning the trip into a checklist. You can spend one day getting to know Cali itself, another exploring nature or the mountains, and a third slowing down and discovering some of the city's food, neighborhoods and nightlife.
</p>

<p>
    This is also where Cali starts to feel different from a quick stop on a Colombia itinerary. You have time to take a salsa class, spend an afternoon in San Antonio, discover a local market, revisit a restaurant you enjoyed, or simply leave a few hours unscheduled.
</p>

<p>
    If you're interested in food and drink, the third day is also a good opportunity to explore some of the culinary traditions of Valle del Cauca through a chocolate experience, coffee excursion or regional food experience.
</p>

<h3>
    Four Days in Cali
</h3>

<p>
    Four days is ideal if you want to add a full-day excursion beyond the immediate surroundings of Cali.
</p>

<p>
    One option is <strong>San Cipriano</strong>, a small Pacific rainforest community famous for its rivers, jungle and unusual motorbike-powered rail carts known as <em>brujitas</em>.
</p>

<p>
    During <strong>whale-watching season, generally from August through October</strong>, you could instead spend the day heading toward Colombia's Pacific coast to look for humpback whales.
</p>

<p>
    Another possibility is a <strong>coffee experience</strong> farther north in Valle del Cauca or toward Colombia's Coffee Region. These trips involve more travel, but can be worthwhile if coffee culture is one of your main interests.
</p>

<h3>
    Five Days or More
</h3>

<p>
    Five days or more is certainly enough to enjoy Cali at a slower pace, but I wouldn't consider it necessary for a first visit.
</p>

<p>
    At this point, you can start exploring some of the city's less conventional experiences: visit <strong>Torre de Cali</strong>, explore community tourism in <strong>Comuna 20</strong>, or spend a day outside the city enjoying water sports such as jet skiing at <strong>Lake Calima</strong>.
</p>

<p>
    The surrounding region also offers activities such as <strong>ATV and buggy excursions, paragliding and other outdoor adventure experiences</strong>.
</p>

<p>
    More time is particularly valuable if you don't like rushing. You can spend a day exploring Cali's neighborhoods, another in the mountains, another discovering regional food and drink, and still have your evenings free for salsa and nightlife.
</p>

<div class="editorial-quote">

<p>
    <strong>Our honest recommendation:</strong>
</p>

<h3>
    One day gives you a taste of Cali. Two days give you a good introduction. Three days is the sweet spot. Anything beyond that is about slowing down or exploring the incredible region around the city.
</h3>

</div>

<p>
    If Cali is part of a larger Colombia itinerary, <strong>three days is an excellent target</strong>. If you only have one or two days, don't skip the city just because you can't stay longer. Cali is worth experiencing even on a short visit — just know that you'll be seeing a concentrated version of it rather than the whole picture.
</p>

<div class="guide-preview">

<h3>
    Looking for Things to Do in Cali?
</h3>

<p>
